\section{Compatible integer towers and p-adic endpoints}\label{sec:towers}
\subsection{The tower is constructed, not supplied}
\contract{$q>0$, $L\ge1$, $F$ has integer coefficients, the starting point is a root modulo $q^L$, and its digit Jacobian over \code{ZMod q} is surjective. The theorem constructs a whole sequence $z_n$ of integer points.}
\excerpt{Strong compatible tower}{tower}
The theorem returns six pieces of information together. It fixes $z_0=x$; $F(z_n)$ vanishes modulo $q^{L+n}$; successive points differ by multiples of $q^{L+n}$; every later point agrees with an earlier point to that earlier depth; the digit Jacobian stays equal to the starting one; and surjectivity holds at every level. These statements keep existence, compatibility and smoothness separate.

