\vpage{1}{One root is not the whole problem}{READER'S GUIDE}{Main text: §§1--3, 8--11. Current contract: Appendix D. Earlier scalar/Mellin checkpoints are historical.}
Chapter X separates three questions that can look deceptively similar. Does a residual object exist? Does it extend coherently through every precision? Does the resulting object occur in the required carrier? A successful answer to one question does not supply the next.
\vfig{01_three_questions}
\head{Three models to keep in view}
The scalar equation $x^2+1=0$ has the root $1$ modulo two, but no root modulo four. Over powers of five, the residues $2,7,57,182$ form the beginning of a compatible branch. Neither fact constructs an automorphic carrier. In a supplied finite carrier, an idempotent such as $\diag(1,0)$ detects an occupied subspace; it does not explain where that carrier came from.

